        heading: "Floodway, Flood Fringe, and the No-Rise Rule",
        body: "The floodway is the river's working channel during a flood, and the regulations treat it accordingly. Any proposed fill, structure, or grading inside the floodway must be supported by a hydraulic analysis, typically built with standard river modeling software, demonstrating zero increase in the base flood elevation at every cross-section, upstream and downstream. That no-rise certification is signed and sealed by the engineer, and reviewers check it closely, because a rise that floods a neighbor upstream is exactly what the rule exists to prevent. Most projects simply avoid the floodway entirely.\n\nThe flood fringe offers more room but no free pass. Buildings here must have their lowest floor elevated to or above the base flood elevation shown on the map, and the engineer documents that elevation with a finished-construction elevation certificate the floodplain administrator keeps on file. Compensatory storage sometimes enters the picture: where fill displaces floodwater storage volume in the fringe, the design excavates an equal volume elsewhere on the site so the floodplain's capacity is unchanged. Freeboard, building a foot or two above the minimum, is the engineer's standard recommendation, since it buys protection against storms bigger than the mapped event and against the map itself being revised upward later.",
