        body: "Elevation is the most reliable flood protection, and the structural design makes it real. Buildings rise on piers, columns, or stem walls engineered for flood loads: hydrostatic pressure, hydrodynamic drag from moving water, buoyancy trying to float the structure, and debris impact. Breakaway walls below the elevated floor are designed to fail cleanly under a defined flood load, sacrificing themselves to protect the main structure. The engineer sizes every element for the flood forces the code assigns, not just gravity and wind, and details connections so the elevated building acts as one unit when water moves against it.\n\nBelow the elevated floor, flood vents do quiet but critical work. Sized by the enclosed area they serve, they open automatically as water rises, letting floodwater enter and exit so pressure equalizes on both faces of the walls. Without them, a few feet of water outside and dry air inside can push a foundation wall to failure. Materials in the flood zone are chosen for wetting: no standard drywall, no fiberglass batts that trap water, just assemblies that can be flooded, cleaned, and dried. Utilities get the elevation treatment too, with electrical panels, HVAC equipment, and water heaters placed above the design flood level, because replacing systems after every flood is not a design.",
