        heading: "Long Spans, Rigging Loads, and the Roof",
        body: "Clear spans over the audience are non-negotiable: nobody pays for a seat behind a column. The engineer spans the room with steel trusses, long-span joists, or glulam beams depending on the architecture, sizing them for the roof loads plus the rigging allowance the venue's business model requires. A club that hosts national touring acts needs a far heavier rigging capacity than a listening room, so the engineer establishes that capacity with the owner early and designs the roof connections, bracing, and support columns for the resulting point loads at any location the rigging grid allows.\n\nThe rigging system itself is engineered, not improvised. Rated attachment points or a structural rigging grid are detailed into the roof framing, each with a documented load capacity, and the venue posts a rigging plot showing touring production managers exactly what they can hang and where. The engineer also accounts for dynamic amplification: a speaker array swaying gently or a scenic piece flying in adds impact beyond its static weight. Periodic re-inspection of rigging steel is specified in the structural documents, because a venue roof accumulates modifications with every show, and undocumented additions are how overloads happen.",
