        body: "Scour analysis drives the foundation depth on every Tennessee River pier. The engineer models the design floods, the 100-year event and the more extreme check flood, computing how deep the current will excavate around each pier considering the pier's shape, the flow angle, and the bed material. That computed scour depth is not where the foundation stops: the deep elements extend well below it into competent material, so that even if the analysis proves optimistic, the pier still stands on rock or on piles with adequate remaining embedment. Streamlined pier noses reduce both the scour and the debris snagging that worsens it.\n\nBuilding the substructure in a working river is its own discipline. Cofferdams, interlocking steel sheet-pile enclosures, are driven around each pier location, sealed, and dewatered so the footing excavation and concrete placement happen in the dry. The design accounts for unbalanced water pressure on the cofferdam, flood events during construction, and environmental containment so concrete wash water and sediments never reach the river. Drilled shafts socketed into rock are verified by inspection of the socket and cleaning of the shaft bottom before concrete placement, because a shaft bearing on cuttings instead of rock is a foundation failure cast in concrete.",
