        question: "What is the urban heat island effect?",
        answer: "It is the tendency of built-up areas to run hotter than surrounding countryside, caused by dark roofs and asphalt absorbing solar energy, sparse vegetation, and waste heat from buildings and vehicles. In Columbia the effect is pronounced because of the hot, humid climate and dense downtown. The island peaks at night, when stored heat radiates back out and keeps overnight lows elevated, which raises cooling demand and heat-related health risk.",
