        body: "Stepped strip footings are the workhorse of hillside residential construction. The footing descends the slope in level steps, each bearing on undisturbed soil, with vertical steps detailed with dowels and keys so the foundation cannot slip at the joints. The structural engineer sizes each step for the wall load above it and checks the global stability of the stepped system against sliding downhill. Where bearing soils are deep or the slope is too steep for stepping, drilled piers socketed into rock carry the loads, with grade beams spanning between piers and resisting the lateral earth pressure from the uphill side.\n\nRetaining walls hold the cuts that make a hillside lot buildable. The engineer selects the wall type by height and conditions: segmental block walls with geogrid reinforcement for moderate heights, soldier pile and lagging where excavation space is tight, or reinforced concrete cantilever walls for the tallest cuts. Every wall is designed for the lateral earth pressure of its retained soil plus surcharge from the house, driveway, or uphill structures -- and every wall gets a drainage system behind it, because water pressure can double the load a wall must resist. Tall walls also need global stability checks to confirm the wall and its retained soil cannot slide as a block.",
