        answer: "Because they typically run 100 percent outside air with 6 to 12 or more air changes per hour -- every cubic foot of supply air must be fully heated, cooled, humidified, or dehumidified from outdoor conditions, with no recirculation. The engineer fights that load with exhaust heat recovery, variable air volume controls that turn airflow down when hoods close or labs empty, and air cascading from offices to labs. Even with these strategies, a lab building uses several times the energy of an equivalent office.",
